[Music]  william shakespeare  considered to be one of the greatest  playwrights ever born he wrote plays  like romeo and juliet  hamlet and a midsummer night's dream but  there's another one of his works that  may have been lost to history  it's called cardinio you won't find it  in a book or on stage  but perhaps in an adaptation of it from  1727  a play going by the name double  falsehood which claims to have preserved  the bard's original script  but is this an elaborate hoax or a  genuine shakespearean play  to be or not to be that is the question  act 1 the curtain closes  we open this story with a death on the  23rd of april 1616  following an illustrious career of  playwriting and poetry  william shakespeare dies aged 52.  now it's not certain how he passed but  the local vicar wrote later  that shakespeare and his friends quote  had a merry meeting  and it seems drank too hard for  shakespeare died of a fever  there contracted during his lifetime  shakespeare did not publish any of his  plays himself  it seems like he expected his  extraordinary works to die with him  or simply fade away from history the  only written record of shakespeare's  plays during his lifetime were through  little pamphlets  called quartos these quarters were small  flimsy pieces of paper  folded twice over to make four leaves  each quarto contained a single play  and while some were quite accurate in  other cases some had rather dubious  contents  these quote unquote bad quartos were  often just written from vague memory  they didn't preserve the play word for  word and the verses were jumbled  lines were missing and other parts of  the play were simply paraphrased  in other words there were cheap  shakespeare knockoffs sold for about a  sixpence  that's about five pounds or six dollars  in today's money  in light of these quote stolen and  superititious copies  a group of shakespeare's friends and  colleagues notably two actors from  shakespeare's dramatic company john  hemmings and henry condell  collected his works into one official  document  being actors for shakespeare himself  they were able to faithfully memorize  much of the plays themselves  but to fill in any gaps they used  whatever they could find any of the good  quartos  prompt books lying about and in some  cases shakespeare's private working  drafts  all of these original documents being  paper are now likely lost to history  but finally some seven years later in  1623  john hemmings and henry condell  published the first  folio a definitive collection of  shakespeare's works  you can think of it as an early  shakespeare canon it contained 36 plays  18 of which had never been officially  published plays like 12th night  macbeth julius caesar and the tempest  had their first folio not been published  these plays might never have survived  needless to say  the first folio is one of the most  important books ever written in the  english language  it contains some of the finest poems and  plays ever written  nowadays if you were to pick up a copy  of the complete works of shakespeare  what you're reading is taken from the  first folio however  the first folio didn't preserve all of  shakespeare's works  the collection is known to emit two of  his plays pericles and edward iii  whether these plays were actually  authored by shakespeare is still heavily  debated  and outside the scope of this video but  both are believed to be partially  written by shakespeare  in fact it was quite common for  playwrights to collaborate in the last  phase of his career  shakespeare collaborated with john  fletcher to produce plays like henry  viii  and two noble kingsmen think of it as a  sort of shakespeare collab  anyway both these texts pericles and  edward iii are now included into the  shakespeare canon  and thus complete the collection well  also you'd think act  2 the lost history of cardinio  in 1653 nearly four decades after  shakespeare's death  a record book called the stationers  register made an entry that shakespeare  put on a play in 1613 going by the name  of the history of cardinio  another clue is the name cardinio  cardinio is a character in the spanish  novel don quixote written by miguel de  cervantes  with an english translation published in  1612 the year before  the history of cardinio was allegedly  performed  if you're unfamiliar with don quixote is  a great story it's all about a man  called alonso who believes that he is a  knight  and he takes the name don quixote and  decides to go on many trafalg  misadventures  meeting many curious characters along  the way one of which  is a man called cardinio in this  particular episode  don and his partner sancho meet this  strange hermit in the mountains  who tells them both a sad story of a  failed love  many years ago cardenia was due to marry  his lover  lucinda for help with this cardino asked  his friend  don ferdinando to arrange a wedding but  when don  ferdinando laid eyes upon lucinda he  decided to steal her from cardinio  at the same time don ferdinando was  already betrothed to a young woman  called dorothea  don ferdinando hatches a plan he sends  cardinio on an errand  and with him out the picture he asks  lucinda's father  for her hand in marriage surprisingly  the father agrees  and the wedding between the two is  underway cardinal returns from his  errand  and stumbles across the wedding hiding  behind a tapestry he watches the event  as it unfolds  at the climactic moment when the vows  are exchanged lucinda  dismayed agrees to marry don ferdinando  and the knot is tied between her  and her treacherous new husband  devastated  cardinio runs away to live in the  mountains yet some time later  lucinda runs away from don ferdinando  and realizing the error of his ways  don fernando finds cardinio and finally  apologizes  cardinio forgives him and then is  reunited with lucinda  finally don ferdinando makes things up  with dorothea  it's this story that could have been the  plot of shakespeare's play that  supposedly happened in 1613.  the date seemed to add up we know that  translation of don quixote was published  in england  in 1612 the year before and so  shakespeare may have had access to the  source material  from which to base his play around as  for an actual play or script  well we don't have it since a copy of it  is not in the first folio  the contents of the play is lost it  would be a miracle  should one of these original quarto  manuscripts ever be found  act three double falsehood  well such a miracle may have happened  our story takes us to the 13th of  december  1727 111 years after shakespeare's death  it was the opening night of a much  discussed play in london  the player's name was double falsehood  and was presented to the public  by louis theobold the title page or the  published version reads as following  written originally by w shakespeare and  now revised and adapted  to stage by mr theobold looking at the  language of the play  and it seems quite shakespearean for an  18th century theatergoer this all would  have been very antiquated and so the  claims made by double falsehood  may indeed seem authentic but the most  intriguing part of the play  is its plot in five acts double  falsehood tells us the story of cardinio  from don quixote  except that now the character's names  have changed  cardinio becomes julio lucinda becomes a  leonora don ferdinando is turned into  henriquez  and dorothea into violente and aside for  some added violence and grit  double falsehood is remarkably similar  to the story of cardinio  so how can we make sense of these two  pieces of evidence  we have this play double falsehood that  tells the story of cardinio  an independent of this we have evidence  that there was indeed a play performed  in 1613  called the history of cardinio this  can't be a coincidence  there must be something more well the  place publisher  louis theobald claimed to have come into  three manuscripts of an unnamed play of  shakespeare that he collected together  and edited into a single work which he  titled double falsehood  he was secretive on where he got those  manuscripts leaving many to believe that  he forged them himself  theobold was a lawyer by trade but in  recent years had become a writer  fascinated with shakespeare and very  familiar with his work  it is entirely possible he had a decent  level of knowledge in which to produce a  plausible forgery of shakespeare  many see double falsehood as an  imitation of shakespeare's work  and a good one at that it uses his  vocabulary and style  either way such claims dogged theobald's  career and the play never took off  he sold the rights away and faded away  from the spotlight  and in the following centuries nobody  really gave any attention to double  falsehood  simply assuming that indeed it probably  was a forgery  but in recent years scholars have  challenged the view that double  falsehood is an out and out forgery  on a closer look at the script it seems  that three distinct writing styles  make up the play three sets of  vocabulary and three  sets of influences these three voices of  double falsehood  the first voice appears most prominently  in acts 1  2 and the first part of act 3. the style  and language  are very close to shakespeare's later  plays the second voice  comes in at acts 4 and 5 except that is  it uses a more modern turn of phrase  instead of hath and doth it prefers has  and does  much like how we'd say it nowadays and  finally the third voice crops up  throughout the play  sometimes in individual sentences and  other times in full paragraphs  it bridges the gaps for the other two  voices a lot of the vocabulary and  phrasing it uses  doesn't really seem very shakespearean  it even references political events  dating it to sometime in the early 1700s  this has challenged the idea that double  falsehood is a simple forgery  if theobald was able to construct three  textual voices in his play  each with a distinct vocabulary in  phraseology then he would have been a  mastermind counterfeiter  which to me and many others seems quite  unlikely  scholars argue that voice three the one  that pissed throughout the play  and bridges the gaps between other  writers is most likely theobolds himself  we know that theobold edited the play  out of an existing manuscript so his  traces in the play  seem to be stitching the whole thing  together that leaves us with voices one  and two  the original voices who wrote the play  voice one  is acts 1 2 and 3 and voice 2 is acts 4  and 5.  so it's believed that acts 1 2 and 3  preserved a text believed to be  shakespeare himself  it's the more antiquated of the two and  many language studies  have linked the vocabulary to a lot of  shakespeare's plays especially those  later ones  and the rest of the play likewise acts 4  and 5 do appear to have  language and phrasing most similar to  john fletcher  so putting it all together acts 1 2 and  3 are shakespeare  acts 4 and 5 john fletcher and the whole  thing is tied together and stitched up  by louis theobald the editor double  holstered preserves the remains of the  original play cardinio as alluded to  in the stationers register in 1653 louis  theobald's bold claim that shakespeare  wrote his play  largely holds up but instead of  shakespeare writing the whole play  it's more that traces and remnants of  shakespeare can be found  within the play especially in the early  half  the only thing we can't be certain of is  how theobold actually got hold of those  three manuscripts  with the text of cardinio he was very  secretive about this precious piece of  evidence  and since we no longer have it today we  must assume that the original cardinio  manuscripts were destroyed  so what's left in double falsehood is  probably our best look at this lost play  of shakespeare  well they have it cardinio the lost  player of shakespeare  i really hope you enjoyed this video i  want to keep experimenting with all the  videos i make  and keep exploring obscure topics that  deserve more attention  so if you'd like to see more why not  subscribe a like and a comment really  help out too  and if you haven't already i'm on  instagram and twitter now i'm aiming to  put some behind scenes content out on  there  anyway i look forward to seeing you next  time goodbye

